oods  and tropical cyclones are all increasing  all the data is showing  that the last few decades they're all  increasing  so are these recent disasters indicators  that we actually  underestimated the speed of climate  change  normally when we make projections it's  over a period of 30 years  we never say that you know what will be  the temperature or the rainfall in 2025  or 2030  we normally say over a period of two to  three decades  this is a likely range but we  models are not good enough to  specifically say  that you know the events that happened  in 2020 or 2021  can be directly linked to you know  climate change  because the limitations of the models  but attribution is increasingly becoming  clear  that many of these events are due to you  know  greenhouse gas concentration increase  and other factors  so what can we do in the short term then  to protect ourselves from the  consequences  well the short term is only adaptation  long term is mitigation which is under  paris agreement you know  reducing emissions but in the short term  you know  one good thing is that due to improved  you know early warning systems and  modeling  satellite information the mortality at  deaths are low even in bangladesh or in  india  let alone in europe or north america but  loss and damage to property houses  infrastructure is  continuous you can't do anything about  it you can't shift the infrastructure  you can shift people during a you know  cyclonic event  so i think the only option is adaptation  you know how we can further improve  early warning systems  how we can provide better housing better  infrastructure how we can incorporate  climate change you know risks into all  our designing buildings  infrastructure so and as a long-term  strategy what needs to be done to tackle  claim to climate change can we do  anything about it  oh in the long term of course we have  the paris agreement you know  it's let us we have some agreement all  countries have agreed  let us st